From: Jeff Johnson <jjohnson@opsero.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:05:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] dmaengine: xilinx_dma: append descriptors to a running SG
 chain

On an AXI DMA in scatter-gather mode the driver starts pending
descriptors only when the channel is idle: xilinx_dma_start_transfer()
returns while chan->idle is false, and the descriptors submitted in the
meantime wait on pending_list until the completion interrupt of the
running chain. xilinx_dma_irq_handler() then sets chan->idle and starts
them (CURDESC, DMACR, TAILDESC) from hard-IRQ context. The engine
therefore stops at the tail of every chain and stays stopped for the
interrupt latency plus the handler's register accesses, even when the
next descriptor was submitted long before.

For a continuous stream into an S2MM channel that gap has to be covered
by a FIFO in front of the DMA. An FPGA design that records a 3.2 GB/s
stream into a ring of 8 MiB buffers (one descriptor per buffer, a new
one always submitted well ahead) measured a 9 us gap at every buffer
boundary on a ZynqMP (Cortex-A53, the IRQ pinned to a CPU of its own),
but over hundreds of GB the restart was occasionally 120-415 us late,
more than a 256 KiB FIFO (82 us at 3.2 GB/s) can absorb: one overflow
every few hundred GB at 2.5-3.2 GB/s. No FIFO size bounds the interrupt
latency of a general-purpose kernel.

The AXI DMA does not need a restart: the SG engine compares each BD it
completes with TAILDESC and keeps fetching along the hardware next
pointers until it has processed the BD at TAILDESC. While the channel is
running (DMACR.RS = 1), a write to TAILDESC moves that pause point; if
the engine has already paused at the old tail (DMASR.Idle = 1) the write
makes it resume, continuing at the old tail's next pointer (PG021,
TAILDESC register description). The bare-metal driver relies on exactly
this: XAxiDma_BdRingToHw() only writes the new tail while the ring runs.

Do the same for an AXI DMA SG channel that is not cyclic:

- xilinx_dma_start_transfer(), channel not idle: new helper
  xilinx_dma_append_running() takes the last BD of active_list (the
  current hardware tail) and the first BD of pending_list. If the tail's
  hardware next pointer (next_desc, plus next_desc_msb with 64-bit
  addressing) is that BD, it writes TAILDESC with the last BD of the last
  pending descriptor, moves pending_list onto active_list and clears
  desc_pendingcount. CURDESC, DMACR (run/stop, coalescing, delay) are not
  touched. The BDs are in coherent memory and were written before the
  descriptor was submitted; xilinx_write() orders the register write
  after them (writel/lo_hi_writeq include the I/O write barrier), as on
  the existing start path.

  The next-pointer test is what makes the append exact: the BD segments
  are pre-linked in ring order at channel allocation and are allocated
  and freed in order, so the test holds in normal operation, but a
  terminate frees the lists out of order (pending, done, active) and
  later descriptors are then not chained to the running tail. If the
  test fails nothing changes: the descriptors stay pending and are
  started by the interrupt handler once the chain has completed, as
  before.

  The append is also done only while the interrupt coalescing threshold
  written by the last start (new field chan->irq_threshold) is 1. The
  start path sets the threshold to the number of descriptors it starts;
  with a larger threshold an appended descriptor could complete without
  reaching it and never be reported. A client that submits and issues
  descriptors one at a time (the case this is for) always runs with
  threshold 1: the first one starts an idle channel with a pending count
  of 1 and the rest are appended. A client that issues a batch keeps the
  old behaviour for that batch.

- xilinx_dma_irq_handler(): for the same channel type, chan->idle is set
  only when xilinx_dma_complete_descriptor() has emptied active_list.
  That function already stops at the first descriptor whose last BD does
  not have the Cmplt bit, so appended descriptors that are still in
  flight stay on active_list, the engine is still running, and it must
  not be restarted from CURDESC. If active_list is empty the engine has
  processed the BD at TAILDESC (TAILDESC is always the last BD of
  active_list) and the old start path applies. The other DMA types, the
  cyclic mode and the non-SG mode keep chan->idle = true.

Races (chan->lock is held by both xilinx_dma_issue_pending() and the
interrupt handler, so they are serialized):

(a) The handler for the completion of the last active descriptor runs
    before the append: it empties active_list, sets idle and starts the
    pending descriptors (if already submitted) the old way, with CURDESC,
    RS and TAILDESC; a later issue_pending finds pending_list empty or an
    idle channel and does the same. Nothing changes compared to today.

(b) The append runs first, whether the engine is still in the old tail
    or already paused there with its interrupt pending: the TAILDESC
    write extends the chain or resumes the engine. The handler for the
    old tail's completion then moves only the finished descriptors to
    done_list; the appended ones lack Cmplt, active_list is not empty,
    idle stays false, and start_transfer() with an empty pending_list
    does nothing. Each appended descriptor raises its own completion
    interrupt (threshold 1); the handler of the last one finds
    active_list empty and sets idle.

(c) Several descriptors complete before one handler run (interrupt
    latency longer than a descriptor): the handler retires all of them
    in order and computes idle from what is left. A completion that sets
    DMASR.IOC_Irq again after the handler's acknowledge produces one more
    interrupt that finds nothing new to retire, as today.

Residue, tx_status, the error path (chan->err set by the handler, the
channel reset on the next submit), terminate and reset are unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Jeff Johnson <jjohnson@opsero.com>
---
 drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c | 94 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 92 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c b/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
--- a/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/xilinx/xilinx_dma.c
@@ -423,6 +423,9 @@
  * @tdest: TDEST value for mcdma
  * @has_vflip: S2MM vertical flip
  * @irq_delay: Interrupt delay timeout
+ * @irq_threshold: AXI DMA interrupt coalescing threshold written by the last
+ *	start (0 = not written yet); descriptors are appended to a running
+ *	chain only while it is 1
  */
 struct xilinx_dma_chan {
 	struct xilinx_dma_device *xdev;
@@ -462,6 +465,7 @@
 	u16 tdest;
 	bool has_vflip;
 	u8 irq_delay;
+	u32 irq_threshold;
 };
 
 /**
@@ -1537,6 +1541,72 @@
 }
 
 /**
+ * xilinx_dma_append_running - Append pending descriptors to a running chain
+ * @chan: Driver specific channel struct pointer
+ *
+ * AXI DMA in scatter-gather mode, non-cyclic: hand the pending descriptors
+ * to an engine that is still processing active_list by moving its tail
+ * pointer, as the bare-metal driver does (XAxiDma_BdRingToHw). The SG engine
+ * compares each BD it finishes with TAILDESC; a new, later tail keeps it
+ * fetching, and if it has already stopped at the old tail (DMASR.Idle with
+ * DMACR.RS still set) the TAILDESC write resumes it. Either way it continues
+ * at the old tail's hardware next pointer; CURDESC, DMACR and RS are left
+ * alone. This removes the hard-IRQ restart (and its latency) from the gap
+ * between two descriptors.
+ *
+ * Done only when the old tail's next pointer is the first BD of the pending
+ * chain (the pre-linked BD ring order, which holds while segments are
+ * allocated and freed in order) and the interrupt coalescing threshold is 1
+ * (one interrupt per completed descriptor, so the appended descriptors are
+ * reported individually). Otherwise nothing changes: the IRQ handler starts
+ * the pending descriptors once active_list has completed.
+ *
+ * Context: called with chan->lock held.
+ * Return: true if the pending descriptors were appended.
+ */
+static bool xilinx_dma_append_running(struct xilinx_dma_chan *chan)
+{
+	struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor *active_tail, *pend_head, *pend_tail;
+	struct xilinx_axidma_tx_segment *active_tail_seg, *pend_head_seg;
+	struct xilinx_axidma_tx_segment *pend_tail_seg;
+
+	if (chan->xdev->dma_config->dmatype != XDMA_TYPE_AXIDMA ||
+	    !chan->has_sg || chan->cyclic || chan->irq_threshold != 1)
+		return false;
+
+	if (list_empty(&chan->active_list) || list_empty(&chan->pending_list))
+		return false;
+
+	active_tail = list_last_entry(&chan->active_list,
+				      struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor, node);
+	pend_head = list_first_entry(&chan->pending_list,
+				     struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor, node);
+	pend_tail = list_last_entry(&chan->pending_list,
+				    struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor, node);
+	active_tail_seg = list_last_entry(&active_tail->segments,
+					  struct xilinx_axidma_tx_segment, node);
+	pend_head_seg = list_first_entry(&pend_head->segments,
+					 struct xilinx_axidma_tx_segment, node);
+	pend_tail_seg = list_last_entry(&pend_tail->segments,
+					struct xilinx_axidma_tx_segment, node);
+
+	/* The engine follows the old tail's next pointer: it must be ours */
+	if (active_tail_seg->hw.next_desc != lower_32_bits(pend_head_seg->phys))
+		return false;
+	if (chan->ext_addr && active_tail_seg->hw.next_desc_msb !=
+	    upper_32_bits(pend_head_seg->phys))
+		return false;
+
+	/* Move the pause point (resumes the engine if it already paused) */
+	xilinx_write(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_TAILDESC, pend_tail_seg->phys);
+
+	list_splice_tail_init(&chan->pending_list, &chan->active_list);
+	chan->desc_pendingcount = 0;
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/**
  * xilinx_dma_start_transfer - Starts DMA transfer
  * @chan: Driver specific channel struct pointer
  */
@@ -1552,8 +1622,15 @@
 	if (list_empty(&chan->pending_list))
 		return;
 
-	if (!chan->idle)
+	if (!chan->idle) {
+		/*
+		 * The engine is still working on active_list. Extend its
+		 * chain if that is safe; otherwise the IRQ handler starts the
+		 * pending descriptors once active_list has completed.
+		 */
+		xilinx_dma_append_running(chan);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	head_desc = list_first_entry(&chan->pending_list,
 				     struct xilinx_dma_tx_descriptor, node);
@@ -1569,6 +1646,7 @@
 		reg |= chan->desc_pendingcount <<
 				  XILINX_DMA_CR_COALESCE_SHIFT;
 		dma_ctrl_write(chan, XILINX_DMA_REG_DMACR, reg);
+		chan->irq_threshold = chan->desc_pendingcount;
 	}
 
 	if (chan->has_sg)
@@ -1918,7 +1996,19 @@
 		      XILINX_DMA_DMASR_DLY_CNT_IRQ)) {
 		spin_lock(&chan->lock);
 		xilinx_dma_complete_descriptor(chan);
-		chan->idle = true;
+		/*
+		 * AXI DMA SG (non-cyclic): descriptors may have been appended
+		 * to the running chain (xilinx_dma_append_running()), and
+		 * xilinx_dma_complete_descriptor() leaves the unfinished ones
+		 * on active_list. The engine has reached its tail only when
+		 * none is left; until then it keeps running and must not be
+		 * restarted from CURDESC.
+		 */
+		if (chan->xdev->dma_config->dmatype == XDMA_TYPE_AXIDMA &&
+		    chan->has_sg && !chan->cyclic)
+			chan->idle = list_empty(&chan->active_list);
+		else
+			chan->idle = true;
 		chan->start_transfer(chan);
 		spin_unlock(&chan->lock);
 	}
